se outline the timing of  the whole process the purchasing and  qualification  ah  okay  so  the lead times  um from  the time that you decide to order a  freezer  and you start the process and you've  never done it before but let's say you  have a the qualification form already in  hand it's pre-approved by quality to do  um if if you really move quick you can  fulfill  the  dq portion of that and get the freezer  ordered  probably uh  inside of a week  depending on how fast your business  moves i mean if you're going to a  manufacturer that supplies all the  information that i talked about the tds  sheets and end-of-line testing and  things like that then that information  is going to be very easy to acquire and  to staple  uh  to the back of that  um  lead times uh you know we're all in hard  times right now i honestly don't know  what the actual manufacturing lead time  is  but that would come into play right now  as well  but by the time you get done doing the  paperwork  and you order the freezer you go through  the qualification aspect of it it  arrives on your dock  you get it qualified  it's not uncommon for this to take  anywhere from four to six months  it can go less  if you have everything in place  everything's ready to go  um  but i've never found that to be the case  in in in my  in in my experience actually so i don't  did i cover all the aspects of that  question  yes i think so  okay yeah thank you  okay next question  can we use 100 ethanol to store our  digital probe  so  sorry i hesitated because that's a very  loaded question  or not  um just for the simple fact  that  um  i can't really comment on putting a  probe in ethanol  i personally have never done it  back years and years ago we would put  our probes in glycol or something  similar to buffer alarms  most folks are getting away from that  now because  most regulations state that you store  the product in the condition of minus 80  so they do not want the condition of  glycol at minus 80 or ethanol  um  so i i have to be honest i can't really  answer that i i i'm not sure which probe  uh if it's the monitoring probe  um common practice in the businesses  that i've been associated with is to put  your monitor monitoring probe in the air  and then if you're worried about  buffering alarms uh during the  qualification you would do an open and  close or open door  study  to simulate  retrieving a sample let's say  and then you would take a look at what  happened to your  freezer temperature  i used to do a one minute open door  study so i would open the door open up  the internal doors  for one minute and then close it  and then i would see did it go back in  spec in 10 minutes did it go out of  specs at all or  and then  i would add a little time  and that way you could set what we would  call alarm delays  uh  in your monitoring system so when when  the unit would go out of spec  um once it went out of spec the clock  would start  what was common on all temps  except for  the ultra cold was a half hour  in my application and that gave us  plenty of time to open and close the  door  uh to get the samples so  hopefully i answered that question  hey dean can i chime in  absolutely  so it might be beneficial when when  you're asking that kind of question to  look at the various standards in the  market one primary example that has been  used at least in the industry for the  last few years has actually been the  uh the epa energy star test procedure  which might be a good reference point as  to how you monitor our or kind of  weighting you would use on your probes  in that standard when we do that  practice it's actually a 50-50 uh glycol  and water solution um the alternate  method to that is using a similar  thermal mass a lot of um a lot of the  industry is going to a copper or a brass  type of  solid material with the same thermal  mass as that 50 50 glycol water solution  effectively that's that that's the  thermal weighting that you're looking  for